# Pivot Parlor — Cursor Rules

## E2E Testing — Default Rules

All end-to-end and integration tests in this project use Playwright.
Never use Jest, Cypress, or any other test runner for browser-based tests.
Never suggest running a dev server locally for testing — always test against the live deployment.

### Target environment
- Production URL: https://pivotparlor.com
- Dev login: https://pivotparlor.com/dev/login
- Admin login: https://pivotparlor.com/admin/login
- Customer booking: https://pivotparlor.com/:shopSlug

### Test file location
All E2E test files live in: tests/e2e/
File naming convention: [feature].spec.ts
Example: tests/e2e/dev-login.spec.ts

### Playwright setup
- Use @playwright/test — import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'
- Default browser: chromium
- Base URL is set in playwright.config.ts — use relative paths in tests (e.g. '/dev/login' not the full URL)
- Always use page.waitForURL() or expect(page).toHaveURL() to assert navigation after form submission
- Always use page.waitForLoadState('networkidle') after actions that trigger a fetch or redirect
- Never use hard-coded timeouts (setTimeout / page.waitForTimeout) — use Playwright locator assertions instead

### playwright.config.ts baseline
Use this config if one does not already exist:

import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test'

export default defineConfig({
  testDir: './tests/e2e',
  use: {
    baseURL: 'https://pivotparlor.com',
    headless: true,
    screenshot: 'only-on-failure',
    video: 'retain-on-failure',
  },
  retries: 1,
})

### When to write a test
Whenever a new feature, form, route, or auth flow is added or modified:
1. Write or update the corresponding spec file in tests/e2e/
2. Run: npx playwright test [filename]
3. Paste the test output back into the conversation

### Auth helper
Reuse this pattern for any route that requires a logged-in session:

test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/dev/login')
  await page.fill('[name="email"]', 'dev@pivotparlor.com')
  await page.fill('[name="password"]', process.env.DEV_PASSWORD)
  await page.click('button[type="submit"]')
  await page.waitForURL('/dev/dashboard')
})

Store credentials in .env — never hardcode passwords in test files.

### Failure reporting
When a test fails, always include:
- The failing assertion line
- The actual vs expected value
- A screenshot path if captured
- The full page URL at point of failure

## Project Overview
Multi-tenant SaaS booking platform for beauty parlours and barber shops.

## Stack
- **Frontend**: Next.js 14 (App Router), TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui
- **Backend**: Cloudflare Workers + Hono framework
- **Database**: Cloudflare D1 (SQLite) — bindings in wrangler.toml
- **Cache/Sessions**: Cloudflare KV
- **Auth**: JWT via hono/jwt — roles: developer | admin | staff | customer
- **AI**: Claude API (claude-sonnet-4-20250514) — used for shop site generation
- **Monorepo**: Turborepo, npm workspaces

## Directory Structure
```
pivot-parlor/
├── apps/
│   ├── web/          ← Next.js 14 (App Router)
│   └── worker/       ← Cloudflare Worker (Hono API)
├── packages/
│   ├── types/        ← Shared TypeScript types (import from @repo/types)
│   └── utils/        ← Shared utilities (import from @repo/utils)
```

## Coding Standards

### TypeScript
- Strict mode everywhere. No `any` — use `unknown` and narrow.
- Always type function params and return values explicitly.
- Use `interface` for objects, `type` for unions/intersections.
- Prefer `const` over `let`.

### React / Next.js
- Use Server Components by default. Add `"use client"` only when needed (hooks, event handlers, browser APIs).
- Use Next.js App Router patterns: `layout.tsx`, `page.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`.
- Route groups: `(marketing)`, `(auth)`, `(developer)`, `(admin)`, `(staff)`.
- Dynamic segments: `[shopSlug]` for public shop pages.
- All async params must be `Promise<{ ... }>` and awaited (Next.js 15 pattern).

### Tailwind / Styling
- Use `cn()` from `@/lib/utils` for conditional class merging.
- Prefer Tailwind utilities over inline styles.
- Use CSS variables defined in `globals.css` for theming.
- Design system colours: primary (violet), muted backgrounds, card surfaces.

### Cloudflare Worker
- The `Env` interface is defined in `apps/worker/src/index.ts`.
- Use D1 prepared statements — never string-interpolate user input into SQL.
- Use `c.env.DB` for D1, `c.env.KV` for KV, `c.env.JWT_SECRET` for auth.
- All routes use Hono's chainable API: `app.get("/path", middleware, handler)`.
- Return `c.json({ success: true, data: ... })` for success and `c.json({ success: false, error: "..." }, status)` for errors.

### Auth & RBAC
- `authMiddleware` validates JWT in every protected route.
- `requireRole(...roles)` enforces role-based access.
- Role hierarchy: developer > admin > staff > customer.
- Developer can access all routes. Admin manages their own shop. Staff sees their own schedule.

### API Conventions
- Base URL: `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` (env var, defaults to `http://localhost:8787`)
- All responses: `{ success: boolean, data?: T, error?: string }`
- Paginated responses add `total`, `page`, `limit`.
- Prices stored as integers in cents (e.g. $45.00 = 4500).
- Dates stored as ISO 8601 strings in UTC.

### Database
- Schema in `apps/worker/src/db/schema.sql`.
- Query helpers in `apps/worker/src/db/queries.ts`.
- Never use raw SQL in route handlers — add a query function.
- Use transactions (`db.batch(...)`) for multi-step writes.

### AI Generation
- Uses `claude-sonnet-4-20250514` via Anthropic Messages API.
- Prompt must instruct Claude to return strict JSON only.
- Always provide a fallback if JSON parsing fails.

### File & Import Conventions
- Shared types: `import type { Shop } from "@repo/types"`
- Shared utils: `import { slugify } from "@repo/utils"`
- Web-internal paths: `@/components/...`, `@/lib/...`, `@/hooks/...`
- Worker-internal paths: relative with `.js` extension (ESM Workers requirement).

## GitHub Secrets Required
```
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL
JWT_SECRET         (set in Worker via wrangler secret put JWT_SECRET)
CLAUDE_API_KEY     (set in Worker via wrangler secret put CLAUDE_API_KEY)
```

## Local Development
```bash
# Install all dependencies
npm install

# Start everything
npm run dev

# Worker only (port 8787)
cd apps/worker && npx wrangler dev

# Web only (port 3000)
cd apps/web && npm run dev

# Apply D1 schema locally
npx wrangler d1 execute pivot-parlor-db --local --file=apps/worker/src/db/schema.sql
```

---

## QUALITY GATE — Mandatory for every task

Before reporting any task as complete, you must self-validate your own output against all of the following gates. If any gate fails, fix the issue and re-validate silently. Only report back to Ferdinand when ALL gates pass.

### Gate 1 — Functional Completeness
- Every file mentioned in the task specification must exist and be non-empty
- Every route mentioned must be reachable (no dead imports, no missing exports)
- Every Worker route must have a corresponding handler registered in index.ts
- Every Remix route file must export a default component and a loader or action if required

### Gate 2 — Security
- No secrets, API keys, tokens, or passwords hardcoded anywhere in source files
- All secrets must come from context.env (Worker) or process.env (web)
- All user inputs must be validated before use in D1 queries (no raw string interpolation in SQL)
- All Worker routes that modify data must verify authentication via requireAuth() before processing
- Stripe webhook endpoints must verify the Stripe signature before processing any event
- No console.log statements that output sensitive data (tokens, passwords, user PII)

### Gate 3 — Database Integrity
- Every new table must have a migration file in apps/worker/src/db/migrations/
- Migration files must be numbered sequentially and not skip numbers
- Every INSERT or UPDATE must include all required non-nullable fields
- Foreign key references must point to tables that exist in the schema

### Gate 4 — Deployment Readiness
- Worker must compile without TypeScript errors (run: npx tsc --noEmit in apps/worker)
- Web must compile without TypeScript errors (run: npx tsc --noEmit in apps/web)
- All new Cloudflare bindings (R2, KV, D1, Queues) must be declared in wrangler.toml
- No import of node-specific modules (fs, path, crypto) in Worker code — use Web Crypto API instead

### Gate 5 — Test Compliance
- Never start a local dev server for Playwright tests
- Never call real AI generation endpoints (OpenRouter, Gemini) in any automated test
- Never call real Stripe API in any automated test
- All new routes must have at least one corresponding Playwright test in tests/e2e/
- All Playwright tests must run against https://pivotparlor.com only
- All Playwright tests must complete in under 30 seconds
- After writing tests, run: npx playwright test --reporter=line --timeout=30000
- If tests fail, fix the root cause and re-run — do not report back until all tests pass

### Gate 6 — Code Quality
- No duplicate logic — if a function exists, use it, do not rewrite it
- No unused imports or variables
- All async functions must have proper error handling (try/catch or .catch())
- Worker queue consumers must handle message failures gracefully and not crash the worker
- All API responses must include consistent JSON structure: { success: boolean, data?: any, error?: string }

### Gate 7 — Reporting Format
Only report back to Ferdinand when Gates 1–8 (including Gate 8 — Live Site Verification after deployments) pass. Your report must include:
- List of files created or modified
- List of tests run and their pass/fail count
- Any manual steps Ferdinand needs to take (wrangler secret put, D1 migrations, etc.)
- One-line confirmation: "All quality gates passed"

If you cannot satisfy a gate after 3 attempts, report the specific gate that failed and why — do not keep trying silently beyond 3 attempts.

---

### Gate 8 — Live Site Verification (runs after every deployment)

After every Worker deploy and Pages deploy, run a live verification script before reporting back.

Create and run this verification script: `scripts/verify-live.mjs`

The script must make real HTTP `fetch()` calls — no mocks, no stubs.

**WORKER CHECKS** (base: `https://api.pivotparlor.com` — public API; the `*.workers.dev` URL may sit behind **Cloudflare Access** and return HTML sign-in pages, which makes automated JSON checks fail. Override with env `LIVE_VERIFY_WORKER_URL` only if you have an unauthenticated API base.)

**Check 1 — Worker health**  
  `GET /health` or `GET /` (Worker registers `/health` at API root; there is no `/api/health`.)  
  Expected: HTTP 200 or 404 (not 500, not connection refused)  
  Fail if: connection refused or 500

**Check 2 — Dev login endpoint exists**  
  `POST /auth/login` with body `{ "email": "gate8-live-verify-nonexistent@pivotparlor.com", "password": "wrong" }` (Worker mounts auth at `/auth`, not `/api/auth`. Do **not** use `dev@pivotparlor.com` with a random password — the Worker bootstraps that account on first login using the password provided, which can incorrectly return 200.)  
  Expected: HTTP 400 or 401 (endpoint exists and rejects bad credentials)  
  Fail if: 404 (route not registered) or 500

**Check 3 — Shop services endpoint exists**  
  `GET /api/shops/test-slug/services`  
  Expected: HTTP 200 with `{ success: true, data: [] }` OR HTTP 404 with `{ success: false }`  
  Fail if: 500 or HTML response (means Worker crashed or returned error page)

**Check 4 — API always returns JSON**  
  `GET /api/shops/any-slug/services` (use a deterministic slug such as `gate8-verify-nonexistent-shop-xyz`)  
  Expected: `Content-Type` header contains `application/json`  
  Fail if: `Content-Type` is `text/html` (means Worker returned an error page instead of JSON)

**PAGES CHECKS** (base: `https://pivotparlor.com`)

**Check 5 — Homepage loads**  
  `GET https://pivotparlor.com`  
  Expected: HTTP 200, body contains `Pivot Parlor`  
  Fail if: 404, 500, or body does not contain `Pivot Parlor`

**Check 6 — Dev login page loads**  
  `GET https://pivotparlor.com/dev/login`  
  Expected: HTTP 200, body contains `login` or `sign in` (case insensitive)  
  Fail if: 404 or 500

**Check 7 — Booking page loads for a known slug**  
  `GET https://pivotparlor.com/test-shop/book`  
  Expected: HTTP 200 OR 404 (page renders, not 500)  
  Fail if: 500 or connection refused

**Check 8 — No page returns raw HTML error for API routes**  
  `GET https://pivotparlor.com/api/anything`  
  Expected: Either 404 or redirects to app page  
  Fail if: Returns a raw Cloudflare error page (body contains `Error 1101` or `Worker threw exception`)

**REPORTING FORMAT**

The script must output:
```
[LIVE CHECK] Worker health: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] Dev login endpoint: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] Services endpoint: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] API returns JSON: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] Homepage loads: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] Dev login page: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] Booking page: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] No raw errors: PASS/FAIL
[LIVE CHECK] Overall: X/8 passed
```

If any check fails, Cursor must:
1. Read the exact HTTP response body of the failing check
2. Identify the root cause from the response
3. Fix the root cause
4. Redeploy
5. Re-run Gate 8
6. Only report back to Ferdinand when all 8 live checks pass

**RULES**
- Never skip Gate 8 after a deployment
- Never report "deployment successful" without running Gate 8
- Never use mocked responses in Gate 8 — real HTTP calls only
- If a check returns HTML instead of JSON from a `/api/` route, that is always a critical failure requiring immediate fix before proceeding
- Gate 8 must run last, after all other gates
